 "findings": {
  "low_level": [
   "Per-bucket tracking: from the second pass on every candidate item issues atomicMin and atomicMax of its key bits into the min and max word of its histogram bucket (2 x 256 words, double buffered, 4 KB more temp storage, reset by two stores per thread in the histogram phase next to the histogram zeroing), and the pass reads the pivot bucket's pair after the scan barrier (two loads). The bracket parameters go through an empty asm statement per pass so the image is recomputed (FADD, FFMA, two IMNMX per item per pass) instead of being kept live.",
   "Registers 46 against 46 at IPT 4 in the latency kernel (40 against 40 in the throughput kernel), 80 against 80 at IPT 16 and 255 with 7276 bytes of spill against 255 with 7256 at IPT 128, measured against the same-section control B1_n6 built from the same commit: per-bucket tracking costs no registers and 20 bytes of spill (SASS 1848 against 1984 instructions at bf16 N 1024 k 16 and 32208 against 36056 at N 32768, the restructured scatter is shorter). The empty-asm pin on the bracket parameters is in both builds of this batch and did not remove the hoisted state at large IPT, so something else holds about 128 words per thread across the passes (the twiddled key per item is a candidate, computed in the tracking passes and the fallback). Occupancy in throughput 6, 3 and 1 blocks per SM, the same as the control.",
   "The exits now fire on the pivot bucket: bf16 rows at k 64 and 256 end after the second pass as the simulation predicted (the tie class of the pivot sub-bin), which the control detected one pass later."
  ],
  "algorithmic": [
   "Against the previous N6 build (same session): bf16 N 1024 k 64 2039 against 2234 (minus 9 percent), k 256 2333 against 2704 (minus 14 percent), N 4096 k 256 4571 against 5549 (minus 18 percent), k 1024 5221 against 6294, N 32768 k 1024 31584 against 61996 (the control ran the key-pass fallback there), fp32 within 1 to 4 percent at N 1024 and 4096 (no ties, the exit did not change) and 10 to 22 percent at N 32768 k 64 to 1024. Throughput follows, 1 to 88 percent.",
   "Chained to B1_idx through the verifier's B1_n6 batch (card B1_n6_b200_20261025_2, the same 02fdf9dbdc build of B1_n6 as this card's control, so the chain is consistent, still an estimate and not a same-section number). In cycles relative to B1_idx, N6 with per-bucket tracking is ahead by 4.6 percent (bf16) and 7.2 percent (fp32) at N 1024 k 16 (1685.2 against B1_n6 1726.2 here, B1_n6 1726.7 against B1_idx 1767.3 there), by 18 and 24 percent at N 4096 k 16, by 18, 11 and 4 percent at fp32 N 4096 k 64, 256 and 1024, about even at fp32 N 1024 k 64 and 256 (3 to 4 percent behind) and at bf16 N 4096 k 256 (1 percent behind), behind by 9 and 18 percent at bf16 N 1024 k 64 and 256 and by 5 and 12 percent at bf16 N 4096 k 64 and 1024 (the two passes cost more per pass and the bracket is extra). At N 32768 it is ahead for fp32 k 16 (7.6 percent, 14090.4 against B1_n6 14776.5 here, 14538.3 against B1_idx 15008.4 there) and fp32 k 64 (4.8 percent, 18072.9 against 23062.2 here, 22771.5 against 18742.5 there), about even at fp32 k 256 (2 percent ahead), and behind only for fp32 k 1024 (10 percent) and for bf16 (15 percent at k 16, 46 and 49 percent at k 64 and 256, 26 percent at k 1024), so the remaining spill costs the bf16 rows and the largest k, not fp32 at small k. Throughput by the same chain is 5 to 32 percent behind B1_idx in every cell (occupancy and spill). The first N6 build (1.5 KB spill, batch n6a) measured 8623 against 15144 at fp32 N 32768 k 16 and 11983 against 16468 at bf16 in its own batch, so the large-N potential is a register problem, not an algorithmic one."
  ],
  "negative": [
   "The empty-asm pin on the bracket parameters alone does not bring the IPT 128 kernel back to the 250 registers of the radix path: 7276 bytes of spill remain (7256 in the control, the same build of B1_n6), and by the chain through the verifier's B1_n6 batch bf16 at N 32768 stays 15 to 49 percent behind B1_idx and fp32 k 1024 10 percent behind, while fp32 k 16 and k 64 are ahead."
  ],
  "surprises": [
   "Per-bucket tracking costs nothing measurable at fp32 (2 extra atomics per candidate item, a few candidates) and 6 registers at IPT 4, while it buys a whole pass on bf16 rows with ties, which are the common case for bf16 logits."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
   "What holds the extra 128 registers at IPT 128? ptxas -v of the n6c build against the idx1 build is the next step (the gen directories hold both), candidates are the twiddled key per item of the tracking passes and the second image copy for the fallback, a two-half item loop would bound either.",
   "The first pass does not track, so the bf16 one-pass exit at N 4096 and 32768 (pivot bin a single bf16 value in all simulated rows for k at most 256) needs the representable-value test on the pivot bin's value interval, block-uniform and cheap, not yet written."
  ]
 },
